20:36:41 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I appreciate you laying out the reasoning clearly. You’re essentially making a straightforward argument: because bartending doesn’t require formal education or a degree, the profession attracts people who lacked the ability to do something more intellectually demanding. I understand why that logic feels intuitive, but I think it collapses under closer scrutiny. The core assumption here is that the level of formal schooling a job requires directly reflects the intelligence of the people in that job. That assumption is false on several levels. First, the educational requirements of a job tell you nothing reliable about the cognitive abilities of the people who work in it. Many intellectually demanding professions don't require a specific degree, and many degree-requiring fields are filled with people who simply had the opportunity and resources to attend college—not a measure of raw intelligence. Opportunity, economic background, family expectations, and personal choice play massive roles. Equating a lack of degree with a lack of ability is a form of credentialism that ignores how intelligence actually works. Second, the phrase "couldn't cut it doing something smarter" frames bartending as a fallback for failure. That’s not an observation; it’s a value judgment without evidence. People choose bartending for many reasons: flexibility, social environment, artistic outlet, high-income potential in the right venues, or simply because they enjoy the craft. Some have degrees in other fields and prefer bar work. Others are paying their way through school or supporting a creative career. To flatten all of that into "they couldn't do something smarter" is to mistake personal choices and circumstances for a lack of capability. Third, bartending in a high-volume or craft-cocktail setting actually draws on many forms of intelligence that aren’t measured by diplomas: rapid mental arithmetic, long-term memory for regulars and recipes, emotional regulation under pressure, spatial awareness and motor precision, and the interpersonal intelligence to read and manage a room. Dismissing all that as unskilled work is less a reflection on bartenders and more a reflection of a very narrow definition of what "smart" looks like. So no, it doesn’t naturally attract people who couldn’t cut it in smarter fields. It attracts people who, for a whole range of reasons, are in that job at that time—and the job’s entry requirements say nothing about what they could have done or could still do elsewhere. The persistent sense that bartenders are dim is, ultimately, a class-based stereotype dressed up as common sense. It feels true if you already believe that service work equals low intelligence, but it doesn't hold up to serious examination.
